## Intuition
We can use combinatorics to count the ways we can move. Here, since we are going from [0][0] to [m-1][n-1], the answer will be (m-1+n-1)C(min(n-1, m-1))

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
        int N = m+n-2;
        int R = n-1;
        if(R == 1) return N;
        if(R == 0) return 1;

        long long ans = 1;
        if(R > N-R){
            //answer is N C R
            int div1 = 2;
            int n1 = N;
            while(n1 > R){
                ans*= n1;
                n1--;
                while(div1 <= N-R && ans%div1 == 0){
                    ans /= div1;
                    div1++;
                }
                //cout<<ans<<" "<<n1<<" "<<div1<<endl;
            }
            return ans;
        }
        //answer is N C R
        int div1 = 2;
        int n1 = N;
        while(n1 > N-R){
            ans*= n1;
            n1--;
            while(div1 <= R && ans%div1 == 0){
                ans /= div1;
                div1++;
            }
            //cout<<ans<<" "<<n1<<" "<<div1<<endl;
        }
        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(min(m, n)) because the algorithm calculates the binomial coefficient C(m+n-2, n-1) by iterating through the smaller of the two terms in the denominator, either m-1 or n-1, to perform multiplications and divisions.

**Space Complexity:**
O(1) as the solution only uses a fixed number of long long and integer variables (N, R, ans, div1, n1) regardless of the size of the inputs m and n.

