## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {
        int left = 0;

        int[] up = new int[n];

        for(int i = 0;i<m;i++){
            for(int j = 0;j<n;j++){
                if(i==0){
                    up[j] = 1;
                    continue; 
                }
                if(j == 0){
                    left = 1;
                    continue;
                }

                int sum = left + up[j];
                left = sum;
                up[j] = sum;
            }
        }

        return up[n-1];
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(m*n)

**Space Complexity:**
O(n).

## Intuition
Understand the use of the previous array. It is mainly used to fetch the top value. When we refer to the prev array from the temp array in the solution mentioned in the editorial, we actually just need the value of the similar index itself from the prev array.
Reference: 
`// Top`
`temp[j] += prev[j]`
This clearly shows that when we are at a given index position in the prev array, *we always know the top value*.

## Approach
We will make use of a single array to mitigate the use of temp array to further optimise the space optimised solution in the editorial.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int uniquePaths(int m, int n) {

        int[] dp = new int[n];

        Arrays.fill(dp, 1);

        for (int i = 1; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j < n; j++) {

                // Left path: dp[j-1];
                // Top path: dp[j];
                // dp[j] is what we refer to for top when using temp array
                // dp[j] = left + top
                dp[j] += dp[j-1];
            }
        }

        return dp[n-1];
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(m*n)

**Space Complexity:**
**O(n)**
Note: Earlier also it was O(n) but as we were using two arrays(prev, temp), so the precise value was **O(2n)**.
However, with this solution it is **exactly O(n)**.

